'''Cotoname'''{{refn|Or '''Cotonamu'''.<ref>{{cite book |last1=Barnes |first1=Thomas C. |last2=Naylor |first2=Thomas H. |last3=Polzer |first3=Charles W. |title=Northern New Spain: A Research Guide |publisher=University of Arizona |url=https://open.uapress.arizona.edu/read/northern-new-spain-a-research-guide/section/1f21260b-5632-4c85-a79f-dde8198f2c5c |access-date=10 May 2024}}</ref>}} is an Indigenous language of Mexico and the American state of Texas formerly spoken by [[Indigenous peoples of the Americas|Native Americans]] [[Classification of indigenous peoples of the Americas|indigenous]] to the lower [[Rio Grande Valley (Texas)|Rio Grande Valley]] of northeastern [[Mexico]] and extreme southern [[Texas]] ([[United States]]). Today it is [[extinct language|extinct]], and is proposed to be a component of a [[Pakawan languages|Pakawan language]] family. Today, it is considered a language isolate.<ref>{{Citation |last=Zamponi |first=Raoul |title=5 Extinct lineages and unclassified languages of Mexico |date=2024-12-02 |work=The Languages and Linguistics of Mexico and Northern Central America |pages=99–158 |editor-last=Wichmann |editor-first=Søren |url=https://www.degruyterbrill.com/document/doi/10.1515/9783110421705-005/html |access-date=2025-06-08 |publisher=De Gruyter |doi=10.1515/9783110421705-005 |isbn=978-3-11-042170-5|url-access=subscription }}</ref>

All known primary witnesses to the Cotoname language were published in 2024.<ref>Haukur Þorgeirsson and Alaric Hall, 'The Cotoname Language – The Primary Sources', ''Zenodo'' (2024), {{doi|10.5281/zenodo.13368224}}.</ref>

